EXT. SKYLINE ROOFTOP BAR - NIGHT

Detective Sergeant Alana Reyes sits at a corner table with a club soda. Across from her, Assistant District Attorney Colm Brennan, in a loosened tie, nurses a bourbon. Neither has touched the appetizers. String lights sway. The city hums far below.

ALANA
(sliding a folder across)
Chain of custody on the Delacroix evidence. Tell me what's wrong with it.

COLM
(not opening it)
Nothing's wrong with it. It's thin, Alana. Thin is not wrong.

ALANA
(quietly)
Thin is how it gets tossed at the suppression hearing.

COLM
(finally flips a page)
Locker 14 was logged out at 2210 and the bag was sealed at 2305. That's fifty-five minutes where a lawyer drives a truck through.

ALANA
(she doesn't flinch)
Officer Pak was on scene the whole time. She'll testify.

COLM
(shaking his head)
She'll testify she was on scene. She won't testify nobody touched it.

A long beat. A waiter passes. Neither speaks until he is gone. Alana picks at the label on her bottle.

ALANA
(low, level)
I need you to file anyway. Monday. Before the defense gets wind of the gap.

COLM
(setting down the glass)
You're asking me to walk into a courtroom knowing I might lose the whole case on a paperwork error.

ALANA
(meeting his eyes)
I'm asking you to walk in knowing the man did it.

COLM
(after a pause, soft)
That's not the standard. You know that's not the standard.

ALANA
(she stands, buttons her jacket)
Then give me the standard. Tell me what I need, and I'll go get it.

COLM
(he looks at the folder, at the city, at her)
Get me Pak's written statement. Notarized. By Friday.

ALANA
(a small nod)
Friday.

She leaves the folder. He pulls it back toward himself and finally, finally starts to read.
